{"hash":"91998cec3c36cf38","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":88.367,"said":"On this western terrace sits the Goddess, Sri Bhramaramba Devi. Here at Srisailam, a Jyotirlinga of Shiva and a Shakti Peetha of the Goddess stand inside the same walls, a rare meeting. Tradition counts this among the eighteen great Shakti Peethas, the holy places where, the old stories say, parts of the body of Sati, Shiva’s first wife, fell to earth; here, it is said, it was her neck. Her name, Bhramaramba, means ‘Mother of the bees’, and the Devi Bhagavata Purana tells why. There was once a demon named Arunasura, who won a clever boon: no creature with two legs or four could ever kill him. He thought himself safe. But the Goddess took the form of Bhramari, and from her poured a vast, humming swarm of bees, creatures with six legs. They fell upon the demon and destroyed him, and then flew back to her. In her temple is a Sri Chakra which, the trust says, was set up by Adi Shankaracharya. Stand still for a moment. Devotees say that if you listen closely, you can hear a faint humming of bees. Now turn back east. Nandi sits waiting before the Lord’s shrine.\n\nDid you know? Every Ugadi, devotees from Karnataka walk hundreds of kilometres through the Nallamala forest to bring a sari to Bhramaramba, whom they honour as their own daughter.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1071},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1087,"end":1252}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,2],[0.29,0.216,3,7],[0.519,0.433,8,15],[0.963,0.421,16,23],[1.396,0.3,24,28],[1.709,0.108,29,32],[1.829,0.505,33,40],[2.502,0.252,42,45],[2.766,0.589,46,57],[3.367,0.457,58,62],[4.712,0.445,64,68],[5.168,0.12,69,71],[5.301,0.769,72,81],[6.262,0.096,83,84],[6.37,0.757,85,96],[7.139,0.18,97,99],[7.332,0.409,100,105],[7.752,0.204,106,109],[7.969,0.048,110,111],[8.029,0.421,112,118],[8.462,0.445,119,125],[8.918,0.144,126,128],[9.075,0.096,129,132],[9.183,0.361,133,140],[9.555,0.409,141,146],[9.976,0.445,147,153],[10.433,0.12,